      "final_answer": "$512\\text{ cm}^2$",
      "mark_breakdown": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Use Pythagoras' theorem to find slant height of the triangular face: $h = \\sqrt{20^2 + 5^2} = 5\\sqrt{17}$"
        },
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Calculate total surface area: area of square base $+ 4 \\times$ area of triangular face"
        },
        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Obtain $100 + 100\\sqrt{17} \\approx 512\\text{ cm}^2$"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "Let the height of the triangles be $h\\text{ cm}$.\nNow $h^2 = 20^2 + 5^2$\n$\\therefore h = \\sqrt{20^2 + 5^2} = 5\\sqrt{17}$\nSurface area $= \\text{area of square} + 4 \\times \\text{area of triangle}$\n$= 10^2 + 4\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 5\\sqrt{17}\\right)$\n$= 100 + 4(25\\sqrt{17})$\n$= 100 + 100\\sqrt{17}\\text{ cm}^2$\n$\\approx 512\\text{ cm}^2$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "The total surface area of a square-based pyramid is the sum of the base area ($b^2$) and the areas of the four triangular lateral faces ($4 \\times \\frac{1}{2} b h$), where $h$ is the slant height of each triangle.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Determine the slant height of the triangular faces using Pythagoras' theorem with the vertical height ($20\\text{ cm}$) and half the base length ($5\\text{ cm}$): $h = \\sqrt{20^2 + 5^2} = 5\\sqrt{17}$.",
          "Compute the area of the square base: $10 \\times 10 = 100\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "Compute the total area of the 4 triangular faces: $4 \\times (\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 5\\sqrt{17}) = 100\\sqrt{17}$.",
          "Sum base and lateral areas: $100 + 100\\sqrt{17} \\approx 512\\text{ cm}^2$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Using the vertical height ($20\\text{ cm}$) instead of the slant height ($5\\sqrt{17}\\text{ cm}$) to calculate the triangle's area."
      },
